    "q-72291": {
      "dom_id": "step-q72291",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14245391/assets/3083c6f4c7998d69857eb7d3a74d905265edd437b5bf73003e55868083993d5a.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the graphs of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$, where $g(x)=f(x+a)+b.$ What are the values of $a$ and $b?$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that every point on the graph $y=g(x)$ is three units *to the left * and two units *down* of the corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x).$ \n\nSo, we conclude that $g(x)=f(x+3)-2,$ and therefore $a=3$ and $b=-2.$",
